2026 3 months ago--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInjector Corrector Power Supplies (https://console.sweetspotgov.com/federal-contracts/712fec73-8e55-5e4e-b42a-9371138b1b11)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; MISCELLANEOUS ELECTRIC POWER AND DISTRIBUTION EQUIPMENT ... This firm-fixed-price subcontract, with solicitation number 365301(RE), is seeking offers due by June 15, 2026, at 5:00 PM PST. ... This certification is a mandatory part of the quotation, requiring the offeror’s signature, date, printed name, title, and company name, ensuring transparency and adherence to domestic sourcing regulations for Solicitation 365301. ... A key instruction for international vendors is to coordinate shipments with SLAC's customs agent, Green Worldwide Shipping (USA), Inc., providing contact details and requiring the \"BSO\" (Broker Select Option) box to be marked. ... The document also details minimum exterior marking requirements for shipping containers, including the addressee (SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory B81, Attn: TBD, 2575 Sand Hill Road, Menlo Park, CA 94025), shipper information, and the SLAC subcontract or purchase order number.\n\nContract Combined Synopsis/Solicitation\n\n# Injector Corrector Power Supplies\n\nDEPARTMENT OF ENERGY• 365301(RE)\n\nResponse Deadline\n\nJun 16, 2026\n\nDeadline passed\n\nDays Remaining\n\n0\n\nClosed\n\nSet-Aside\n\nFull & Open\n\nNotice Type\n\nCombined Synopsis/Solicitation\n\n### Contract Opportunity Analysis\n\nSLAC National Accelerator Laboratory, operated by Stanford University for the U.S. Department of Energy, is seeking a firm-fixed-price subcontract for Injector Corrector Power Supplies to replace an obsolete and unreliable SPEAR injector corrector power supply system. The work calls for 91 power supply modules with crates, an EPICS-compatible control system interface, remote technical support, and associated documentation, testing, and reports. The supplies must meet specified electrical and safety requirements, including 120VAC input, ±20V and ±6A output, high current stability, and NRTL certification or comparable safety compliance, with technical acceptability, milestones, and past performance driving evaluation under a lowest-price-technically-acceptable approach. Proposals are due June 15, 2026 at 5:00 PM, clarification questions were due June 9, 2026, and offerors must acknowledge Amendment 01 and submit the package to Ryan Enos for performance in Menlo Park, California.\n\n### Classification Codes\n\nNAICS Code\n\n334419\n\nOther Electronic Component Manufacturing\n\nPSC Code\n\n6150\n\nMISCELLANEOUS ELECTRIC POWER AND DISTRIBUTION EQUIPMENT\n\n### Solicitation Documents\n\n22 Files\n\nSearch documents[Input: Search files...]\n\nAll 22 PDF 21 Word 1\n\nRFP_Section_L_Instructions to Offerors (B2).pdf\n\nPDF 194 KB Jun 4, 2026\n\nThis government file, Section L – Instructions to Offerors for Firm Fixed Price Subcontract, outlines the requirements for preparing proposals for \"Injector Corrector Power Supplies_RFP_365301(RE).\" Proposals are due by June 15, 2026, at 5:00 PM PST and must be emailed to Ryan Enos, the sole point of contact. Key technical requirements include modules with specific voltage, current, stability, and compatibility with the EPICS control system. Proposals must be submitted in two volumes: Volume I (Technical Proposal, 35-page limit) detailing technical ability, milestones, performance, and past experience; and Volume II (Business Proposal) containing financial information and pricing. Business proposals are assessed on financial/organizational health (SER score), and completeness and reasonableness of price, which is compared against SLAC’s estimates. Debriefings are available within three days of an unsuccessful award notification to explain evaluation rationale, but not to compare proposals.\n\n00_RFP_365301(RE)_CoverLtr_Injector Corrector PS.pdf\n\nPDF 341 KB Jun 4, 2026\n\nSLAC National Accelerator Laboratory, operated by Stanford University for the U.S. Department of Energy, has issued Request for Proposal (RFP) 365301(RE) for Injector Corrector Power Supplies. Proposals, on a firm-fixed price basis, are due by June 15, 2026, 5:00 PM Pacific, and must be valid for 90 days. Offerors must agree to all Terms and Conditions, provide detailed cost proposals, and demonstrate cost reasonableness. Proprietary information must be marked. Domestic offerors need to register with the System for Award Management (SAM.gov), while foreign entities are exempt. Required documents include the Subcontract Award Form, Supplies & Services breakdown, representations and certifications (including Buy American Act Certification), and an Offeror’s Qualification Matrix Checklist. Financial stability may require audited statements. Exceptions to SLAC's terms must be clearly identified. Ryan Enos, Sr. Subcontract Administrator, is the sole point of contact for all inquiries, which must be submitted in writing by June 9, 2026.\n\nRFP_Section_A_(Subcontract_Form)_07.08.22_(B2).pdf\n\nPDF 221 KB Jun 4, 2026\n\nThe SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory, operated by Stanford University for the U.S. Department of Energy, has issued a Subcontract Form (SLAC-SCM-GEN-028) for the procurement of Injector Corrector Power Supply Systems. This firm-fixed-price subcontract, with solicitation number 365301(RE), is seeking offers due by June 15, 2026, at 5:00 PM PST. The acquisition aims to secure 91 power supply modules and associated crates. The subcontract outlines various sections including the award form, supplies/services, specifications, delivery, inspection, terms and conditions, and instructions to offerors. The total amount will be detailed in Section B, and the agreement is governed by the subcontract award, solicitation, and incorporated clauses. \nOfferors_Request_for_Clarifications.pdf\n\nPDF 156 KB Jun 4, 2026\n\nThe document,\n\nAmd_01_RFP_365301(RE)_6.4.26.pdf\n\nPDF 261 KB Jun 4, 2026\n\nAmendment 01 to Solicitation/Subcontract Number 365301, issued by The Board of Trustees of the Leland Stanford, Jr. University for the SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory, modifies the original solicitation. Effective June 4, 2026, this amendment incorporates the Technical Team's responses to vendor clarifications into the Request for Proposal (RFP) package. Specifically, it includes the document titled \"Offeror's Request for Clarifications_SLAC_Responses_6.4.2026.\" The deadline for receipt of offers has been extended to June 15, 2026, at 5:00 PM. All offerors are required to acknowledge receipt of this amendment, as failure to do so may result in the rejection of their offer. All other terms and conditions of the original solicitation remain unchanged.\n\nRFP_Section_J_Attachment 01_Tax Exempt and Resale Cert_OCT2025.pdf\n\nPDF 456 KB Jun 4, 2026\n\nThe SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory (SLAC), a Federally Funded Research and Development Center managed by Leland Stanford Jr. Price evaluation in Section B requires firm fixed prices and a price certification, ensuring SLAC receives the best price.\n\nRFP_Section_L_Attachment 02_Offeror's Request for Clarifications.doc\n\nWord 74 KB Jun 4, 2026\n\nThis document, \"ATTACHMENT 02 OFFEROR’s REQUEST FOR CLARIFICATIONS,\" is part of Request for Proposal No: SLAC_RFP_365301(RE) for \"Injector Corrector Power Supplies.\" It serves as a form for offerors to submit questions and clarification requests regarding the RFP. The form requires the offeror's name and date, and provides a structured table with columns for item number, submission date, RFP document reference, question, response date, response, and RFP actions. Offerors are instructed to complete and email this form to the Sr. Subcontract Administrator, Ryan Enos, at ryane@slac.stanford.edu by June 9th, 2026, 5pm PST. The primary purpose of this attachment is to facilitate a clear and transparent communication channel between the issuing agency (SLAC) and potential offerors, ensuring all parties have a complete understanding of the proposal requirements before final submissions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElectronic Journal of Linear Algebra, ISSN 1081-3810 (https://journals.uwyo.edu/index.php/ela/article/download/9645/7397/29091)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; To investigate this conjecture, in Subsection 3.4, we study how the contact order of pA at (−1, 1) changes ... resolved by Adlin, Thai, Tiscarenoby, and Tully-Doyle using very interesting and eﬃcient methods in the\nElectronic Journal of Linear Algebra, ISSN 1081-3810\nA publication of the International Linear Algebra Society\nVolume 42, pp. 213-238, March 2026.\nK. Bickel and Y. Hong\n218\nConjecture 1.4. Let GA be an n-vertex graph and let ℓdenote the length of the shortest path connecting\nvertices v1 and vn in GA. If t = 0, then pA has contact order 2ℓat (−1, 1) and if t ∈(0, 1), then pt\nA has\ncontact order 2ℓ+ 2 at (−1, −1).\nTo investigate this conjecture, in Subsection 3.4, we study how the contact order of pA at (−1, 1) changes\nwhen we append additional vertices and edges to the graph GA. The results are summarized in the following\ntheorem:\nTheorem 1.5. Let GA be an n-vertex graph such that pA has contact order K at (−1, 1).\ni. Create G b\nA from GA by adding a vertex vn+1 that is only connected directly to vn. Then, p b\nA has\ncontact order K + 2 at (−1, 1).\nii. Create G e\nA from GA by adding a vertex v0 that is only connected directly to v1 (and then renumbering\nto v1, . . . , vn+1). Then, p e\nA has contact order K + 2 at (−1, 1).\niii. Assume that vn is only connected to the rest of GA by an edge to vn−1. For m ≥n + 1, create an\nm-vertex graph G ˘\nA from GA by attaching an (m −n)-vertex graph G′ to GA via an edge to vn−1\nand then relabeling vn to vm. Then, p ˘\nA has contact order K at (−1, 1).\nNote that all three situations in Theorem 1.5 support Conjecture 1.4. In the ﬁrst two situations, we\nextend the length of the shortest path connecting the ﬁrst and last vertices by 1; thus, it makes sense that\nthe contact order increases by 2. In the last situation, the length of the shortest path connecting the ﬁrst\nand last vertices does not change, so we would expect the contact order to remain the same. Theorem 1.5\nis proved as three separate results (Theorem 3.5, Theorem 3.6, and Theorem 3.7), where Theorem 3.7 also\nallows the option of constructing G ˘\nA by attaching G′ to both vn−1 and vn.\nIt is worth noting that since the completion of this work, the t = 0 part of Conjecture 1.4 has been\nresolved by Adlin, Thai, Tiscarenoby, and Tully-Doyle using very interesting and eﬃcient methods in the\nnew paper [1]. The t ̸= 0 case appears to still be open.\nFinally, Section 4 contains a short list of open questions related to stable polynomials and graphs. Of\nparticular interest to the authors are investigations that go in the other direction: given a stable polynomial\nor Cauchy transform function associated to a colored graph, what can one say about the original graph? To\nthe best of our knowledge, little in that direction is currently known.\n2. Existence of boundary zeros.\n2.1. Key lemmas. In this section, we record some facts about inverses, determinants, and the functions\nf t\nA deﬁned in (1.7) that will be used later.\nLemma 2.1. If A is a square matrix and A is the matrix obtained from A by removing its ﬁrst row and\ncolumn, then the (1, 1) entry of A−1 is given by\n(A−1)11 = det A\ndet A.\nThis is an immediate consequence of the adjugate formula for A−1. We will also require the following.\nLemma 2.2. Let M be a square matrix, partitioned as\n#\nM =\n,\n\"\nA\nB\nC\nD--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nManuel Télérecours Avocats adhérents au RPVA (https://www.conseil-etat.fr/content/download/193968/file/28df97a00be34ceff3bca54a2a4876b1.pdf